§§ `The helper that runs a rule (and what CONSIDER EVALTRACE can tell apart on l4 1.6.98)`

-- s 18 notice of 14 days from day 100: done on day 114 (the last day) kept; day 115 a breach, read as not kept.
#ASSERT `the outcome of` (`s 18(2), (5) -- the notice recipient must ensure the graffiti is obliterated within the time in the notice` 14 FALSE) `the notice recipient` (`ensure the graffiti is obliterated` `in a manner acceptable to the local government`) 100 TRUE 114 `an unasked number` EQUALS `the rule was kept`
#ASSERT `the outcome of` (`s 18(2), (5) -- the notice recipient must ensure the graffiti is obliterated within the time in the notice` 14 FALSE) `the notice recipient` (`ensure the graffiti is obliterated` `in a manner acceptable to the local government`) 100 TRUE 115 `an unasked number` EQUALS `the rule was not kept`
-- Not done: on day 114 still open; on day 115 the time has run out (a breach without an excuse) ...
#ASSERT `the outcome of` (`s 18(2), (5) -- the notice recipient must ensure the graffiti is obliterated within the time in the notice` 14 FALSE) `the notice recipient` (`ensure the graffiti is obliterated` `in a manner acceptable to the local government`) 100 FALSE `an unasked number` 114 EQUALS `the rule is still open`
#ASSERT `the outcome of` (`s 18(2), (5) -- the notice recipient must ensure the graffiti is obliterated within the time in the notice` 14 FALSE) `the notice recipient` (`ensure the graffiti is obliterated` `in a manner acceptable to the local government`) 100 FALSE `an unasked number` 115 EQUALS `the rule was not kept`
-- ... and with an excuse (no breach: the s 19 rule waits instead) it is still not kept.
#ASSERT `the outcome of` (`s 18(2), (5) -- the notice recipient must ensure the graffiti is obliterated within the time in the notice` 14 TRUE) `the notice recipient` (`ensure the graffiti is obliterated` `in a manner acceptable to the local government`) 100 FALSE `an unasked number` 115 EQUALS `the rule was not kept`
-- A plain CONSIDER of a breached trace with OTHERWISE is accepted (no error), but cannot say BREACH: the breach on day
-- 20 and the duty still pending on day 10 read alike.
GIVEN `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A STRING
`what a plain CONSIDER says of the 14-day notice on` `the day` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 18(2), (5) -- the notice recipient must ensure the graffiti is obliterated within the time in the notice` 14 FALSE) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN "fulfilled"
    OTHERWISE "not fulfilled"

#ASSERT `what a plain CONSIDER says of the 14-day notice on` 20 EQUALS "not fulfilled"
#ASSERT `what a plain CONSIDER says of the 14-day notice on` 10 EQUALS "not fulfilled"

§§ `For the person who put graffiti on property, or is accused of it`

-- The situation (D8) is what I am accused of: s 5, s 6 or both. The s 5 questions do not arise for s 6 alone, and the
-- s 6 questions not for s 5 alone; nothing else is asked then. Forfeiture takes no situation input.

-- Is it an offence for me to put this mark on this property (ss 3, 5)?
-- Spray-painting a neighbour's fence without asking: yes. With the neighbour's consent: no.
#ASSERT `Is it an offence for me to put this mark on this property` `as the accused` `accused under s 5` `spraying` TRUE FALSE EQUALS yes
#ASSERT `Is it an offence for me to put this mark on this property` `as the accused` `accused under s 5` `spraying` TRUE TRUE EQUALS no
-- On my own wall: no, and nobody's consent is asked.
#ASSERT `Is it an offence for me to put this mark on this property` `as the accused` `accused under s 5` `spraying` FALSE `an unasked fact` EQUALS no
-- Carving initials into a council tree (s 5(2) vegetation, scratching or etching), accused under both sections: yes.
#ASSERT `Is it an offence for me to put this mark on this property` `as the accused` `accused under both` `etching` TRUE FALSE EQUALS yes
-- Sticking a poster on a shopfront is not graffiti (s 3): no, and nothing else is asked.
#ASSERT `Is it an offence for me to put this mark on this property` `as the accused` `accused under s 5` `a sticker` `an unasked fact` `an unasked fact` EQUALS no
#ASSERT `Is it an offence for me to put this mark on this property` `as the accused` `accused under s 6` `an unasked way` `an unasked fact` `an unasked fact` EQUALS `does not arise for you`
